# Great spotted woodpecker

The great spotted woodpecker is the common pied woodpecker of Europe and
northern Asia, black and white with a crimson vent. It takes wood-boring larvae
by chiselling into dead timber and extracting them with a long barbed tongue, and
in winter wedges cones into a bark crevice to work the seeds out. Its drumming,
a short accelerating burst delivered against a resonant dead limb, carries
several hundred metres. The cavity is excavated fresh each year, and old ones
are taken over by tits, starlings and bats. See [[picidae]].
